SELECT set_config('request.jwt.claim.role','service_role',false);
SELECT set_config('request.jwt.claim.sub','f49d6153-1835-4455-81f3-2918d5d3484e',false);
BEGIN;
INSERT INTO public.qb_questions (subject_id, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, points, tags) VALUES
('3c09b2b2-10bd-4e1e-a51d-661d013948bc', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A production engineer calculates that automating a specific high-volume operation would pay back its capital cost in 14 months based on current order volume. However, the FACTORY''s overall order book shows this specific style''s volume is expected to decline by 40% after this current season (a planned product lifecycle transition). How should this additional information change the automation investment decision?', '["The additional volume-decline information is irrelevant to the automation decision, since a 14-month payback period is always automatically attractive regardless of any future volume change","The payback period calculation should be RECALCULATED using the REALISTIC future volume trajectory (not just current volume), since a 40% volume decline after this season would substantially LENGTHEN the effective payback period beyond the original 14-month estimate (which implicitly assumed CURRENT volume continuing) — this connects to the earlier automation-investment-justification principle (requiring genuine sustained high volume), and a declining-volume style is a weaker candidate for automation investment than the initial current-volume-only payback calculation suggests","Declining future volume always makes an automation investment MORE attractive, the opposite of the correct relationship between future volume trajectory and automation payback economics","Payback period calculations should never account for future volume projections under any circumstances, relying exclusively on current, present-moment order volume regardless of known future changes"]'::jsonb, 'The payback period calculation should be RECALCULATED using the REALISTIC future volume trajectory (not just current volume), since a 40% volume decline after this season would substantially LENGTHEN the effective payback period beyond the original 14-month estimate (which implicitly assumed CURRENT volume continuing) — this connects to the earlier automation-investment-justification principle (requiring genuine sustained high volume), and a declining-volume style is a weaker candidate for automation investment than the initial current-volume-only payback calculation suggests', 'This directly extends the earlier automation-investment-justification principle with a critical refinement — a payback calculation based on CURRENT volume alone can be seriously misleading if that volume isn''t expected to CONTINUE at the same level; since automation investment specifically requires genuine SUSTAINED high volume to be justified (the whole basis of the earlier justification principle), a known, planned 40% volume decline after this season means the REALISTIC payback period is meaningfully longer than the current-volume-only calculation suggests, making this specific investment decision weaker than it initially appears — a sophisticated capital allocation decision must incorporate known future volume trajectory, not just a snapshot of current order volume.', 3, '{"automation-payback-volume-trajectory"}'::text[]),
('3c09b2b2-10bd-4e1e-a51d-661d013948bc', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A technical designer specifying a garment''s interlining fusing parameters (temperature, pressure, dwell time) for a NEW fabric composition (a novel fiber blend the factory hasn''t previously fused) insists on running INCREMENTAL fusing trials (testing multiple temperature/pressure/time combinations on small swatches) rather than simply applying the factory''s ''standard'' fusing recipe used for other, different fabrics. What is the technical justification for this extra trial step?', '["Incremental fusing trials are an unnecessary waste of time; a single standard fusing recipe always works identically well across every possible fabric composition with zero risk","Different fiber compositions have different HEAT SENSITIVITY, melting/softening behavior, and surface characteristics (connecting to the earlier fusible-interlining-selection discussion about heat-sensitive fabrics risking scorching or inadequate bond) — a standard recipe optimized for a DIFFERENT fabric composition carries real risk of either insufficient bond strength (too little heat/pressure/time for this specific new fabric) or fabric damage (too much heat for this specific new fabric''s sensitivity), so incremental trials on the ACTUAL new fabric composition are needed to find genuinely appropriate parameters rather than assuming a standard recipe transfers safely","Fusing parameters have no relationship to fabric composition, making fiber-composition-specific trials technically unnecessary for any fusing application","Incremental trials are only relevant for garment SEWING parameters, never for fusing/bonding processes, making this entire trial-testing concept technically inapplicable to interlining fusing"]'::jsonb, 'Different fiber compositions have different HEAT SENSITIVITY, melting/softening behavior, and surface characteristics (connecting to the earlier fusible-interlining-selection discussion about heat-sensitive fabrics risking scorching or inadequate bond) — a standard recipe optimized for a DIFFERENT fabric composition carries real risk of either insufficient bond strength (too little heat/pressure/time for this specific new fabric) or fabric damage (too much heat for this specific new fabric''s sensitivity), so incremental trials on the ACTUAL new fabric composition are needed to find genuinely appropriate parameters rather than assuming a standard recipe transfers safely', 'This directly extends the earlier fusible-interlining-selection discussion (heat-sensitive fabrics, witness marking risk) with a systematic process-development justification — since different fiber compositions have genuinely different heat sensitivity, softening/melting behavior, and surface characteristics, a fusing recipe validated on a DIFFERENT fabric composition provides no real guarantee of appropriate performance on a genuinely NEW composition — incremental trials (testing a range of parameter combinations on small swatches of the ACTUAL new fabric) is the technically correct approach to empirically find safe, effective parameters for that specific new material, rather than assuming any ''standard'' recipe transfers safely across fundamentally different fabric compositions.', 3, '{"fusing-trial-methodology"}'::text[]),
('3c09b2b2-10bd-4e1e-a51d-661d013948bc', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A garment technologist explains that ''seam grin'' (a defect where the seam allowance/stitching becomes visible THROUGH the fabric, especially under stretch or at certain viewing angles) is a DIFFERENT defect from ''seam slippage'' (yarns physically shifting apart at the seam), even though both can make a seam look problematic. What is the KEY structural distinction between these two defects?', '["Seam grin and seam slippage are actually identical defects with two different names, making any structural distinction between them technically meaningless","Seam slippage involves ACTUAL yarn movement/separation within the fabric structure near the seam (a genuine structural failure, as discussed earlier), while seam grin is primarily a VISUAL/OPTICAL issue where the seam''s construction becomes visible through the fabric (often due to insufficient fabric opacity/cover at the stitched area, or the seam allowance''s bulk creating a visible show-through under stretch) WITHOUT necessarily involving any actual yarn displacement — grin can occur even in a structurally sound, non-slipping seam if the fabric''s cover/opacity is simply inadequate at that specific point","Seam grin only occurs in KNITTED fabric, while seam slippage only occurs in WOVEN fabric, making fabric type (not the actual defect mechanism) the real distinguishing factor between the two","Seam grin is always more severe and costly to fix than seam slippage in every single case, making severity level the only meaningful distinction between the two defect types"]'::jsonb, 'Seam slippage involves ACTUAL yarn movement/separation within the fabric structure near the seam (a genuine structural failure, as discussed earlier), while seam grin is primarily a VISUAL/OPTICAL issue where the seam''s construction becomes visible through the fabric (often due to insufficient fabric opacity/cover at the stitched area, or the seam allowance''s bulk creating a visible show-through under stretch) WITHOUT necessarily involving any actual yarn displacement — grin can occur even in a structurally sound, non-slipping seam if the fabric''s cover/opacity is simply inadequate at that specific point', 'This extends the earlier seam-slippage discussion by distinguishing it from a related but mechanistically DIFFERENT defect — seam slippage is a genuine STRUCTURAL failure (yarns physically moving/separating within the fabric near the seam, connecting to the earlier low-density-fabric seam-slippage risk discussion), while seam grin is primarily a VISUAL/OPTICAL phenomenon (the seam construction becoming visible through the fabric, especially under stretch, due to inadequate fabric OPACITY/cover at that specific point) — critically, grin can occur in a fabric with NO actual yarn slippage at all, purely from insufficient cover factor (connecting to the earlier opacity-vs-structure discussion), meaning the two defects require genuinely different root-cause diagnosis and correction approaches despite both making a seam ''look wrong.''', 3, '{"seam-grin-vs-slippage"}'::text[]);
COMMIT;
